-8.344646857949556e-07,-0.3899745047092438,-4.529942543740617e-06,0.0,0.0,-7.152555099310121e-07,-7.152555099310121e-07,-2.145764938177308e-06,-9.536738616588991e-07],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,32,2311,448,220,20,15,6816,48826,220,16,1526,220,20,15,702,1181,6816,312,4082,291,304,9931,11,504,220,20,15,311,220,16,13,1752,1246,1657,6816,653,2176,7289,315,2150,5109,4332,279,1852,6174,15723,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA book with 50 pages numbered 1 through 50 has its pages renumbered in reverse, from 50 to 1. For how many pages do both sets of page numbers share the same ones digit?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"85911905485e90de7a2e55d60a6a0eeea777b69fe81623cab7eb4fb2fbd79cff"},"seconds":11.965648651123047,"seed":736428634496089252,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine how many pages have the same ones digit in both the original and the reversed numbering, we need to compare the ones digit of the original page numbers (1 through 50) with the ones digit of the reversed page numbers (50 through 1).\n\nLet's denote the original page number by \\( n \\) and the reversed page number by \\( 51 - n \\). We need to find the values of \\( n \\) such that the ones digit of \\( n \\) is the same as the ones digit of \\( 51 - n \\).\n\nThe ones digit of a number \\( n \\) is the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 10. So, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\\[ n \\equiv 51 - n \\pmod{10} \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 2n \\equiv 51 \\pmod{10} \\]\nSince \\( 51 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10} \\), we have:\n\\[ 2n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10} \\]\nTo solve for \\( n \\), we need the multiplicative inverse of 2 modulo 10, which is a number \\( x \\) such that \\( 2x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10} \\). By checking, we find that \\( x = 5 \\) works because:\n\\[ 2 \\cdot 5 = 10 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{10} + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10} \\]\nSo, we multiply both sides of \\( 2n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10} \\) by 5:\n\\[ n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{10} \\]\nThis means \\( n \\) must be of the form \\( 10k + 5 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). The possible values of \\( n \\) within the range 1 to 50 are 5, 15, 25, 35, and 45. There are 5 such values.\n\nThus, the number of pages that have the same ones digit in both the original and the reversed numbering is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:364"}
